  "segments": [
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "In the kitchen, Max is bored and looking for a distraction. He approaches Chef Victor and curiously asks about the special dish the chef is preparing, but Victor quickly shuts him down. Let us listen to their conversation in Russian first.",
      "gap": 0.8
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Шеф.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "leva",
      "t": "Что тебе надо?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "А что вы готовите?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "leva",
      "t": "А тебе какое дело?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "leva",
      "t": "Тебе нечем заняться?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "У меня есть пять минут.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Я хотел поучиться у вас.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "leva",
      "t": "Иди работай.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "А можно попробовать?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "leva",
      "t": "Нет, нельзя.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Почему?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "leva",
      "t": "Потому что я так сказал.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Ладно, я понял.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "leva",
      "t": "Тогда иди отсюда.",
      "gap": 0.9
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "Now let us examine the conversation line by line.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Шеф.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Chef.' — Built around the key proper name meaning 'chef, boss'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "leva",
      "t": "Что тебе надо?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'What do you need?' Russian omits the present-tense verb 'to be', connecting 'what' directly with 'it is necessary'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "А что вы готовите?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'And what are you preparing?' Max combines 'and' with 'to cook' in a natural Russian turn of phrase.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "leva",
      "t": "А тебе какое дело?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'And what is it to you?' Viktor uses the casual singular 'you' with 'for you', as close colleagues and friends do.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "leva",
      "t": "Тебе нечем заняться?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Do you have nothing to do?' Chef Viktor combines 'for you' with 'to attend to' in a natural Russian turn of phrase. Then Max responds.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "У меня есть пять минут.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I have five minutes.' Notice how 'at' sets up the clause and leads naturally into 'minutes'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Я хотел поучиться у вас.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I wanted to learn from you.' This everyday Russian sentence pairs 'I' with 'you'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "leva",
      "t": "Иди работай.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Go work.' Chef Viktor links 'go' directly with 'work' to express the thought clearly.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "А можно попробовать?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'And can I try it?' This everyday Russian sentence pairs 'and' with 'to taste'. Then Chef Viktor responds.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "leva",
      "t": "Нет, нельзя.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'No, you cannot.' Chef Viktor links 'no' directly with 'not allowed' to express the thought clearly.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Почему?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Why?' Max asks this with a single word meaning 'why', using rising intonation to turn it into a quick question.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "leva",
      "t": "Потому что я так сказал.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Because I said so.' The question word for 'what' comes right at the start, followed directly by 'said'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Ладно, я понял.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Alright, I understand.' Max combines 'all right' with 'understood?' in a natural Russian turn of phrase. Then Chef Viktor responds.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "leva",
      "t": "Тогда иди отсюда.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Then go from here.' This everyday Russian sentence pairs 'back then' with 'from here'. Now let us listen to the conversation once again.",
      "gap": 0.9
    },
